{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAlice and Bob have decided to play the game \"Rock, Paper, Scissors\". The game consists of several rounds, each round is independent of each other. In each round, both players show one of the following things at the same time: rock, paper or scissors. If both players showed the same things then the round outcome is a draw. Otherwise, the following rules applied:  if one player showed rock and the other one showed scissors, then the player who showed rock is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser;  if one player showed scissors and the other one showed paper, then the player who showed scissors is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser;  if one player showed paper and the other one showed rock, then the player who showed paper is considered the winner and the other one is considered the loser. Alice and Bob decided to play exactly $$$n$$$ rounds of the game described above. Alice decided to show rock $$$a_1$$$ times, show scissors $$$a_2$$$ times and show paper $$$a_3$$$ times. Bob decided to show rock $$$b_1$$$ times, show scissors $$$b_2$$$ times and show paper $$$b_3$$$ times. Though, both Alice and Bob did not choose the sequence in which they show things. It is guaranteed that $$$a_1 + a_2 + a_3 = n$$$ and $$$b_1 + b_2 + b_3 = n$$$.Your task is to find two numbers:  the minimum number of round Alice can win;  the maximum number of rounds Alice can win. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class rps {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String[] firstLine = br.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(firstLine[0]);\n        String[] secondLine = br.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int[] game = new int[6];\n        for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {\n            game[i] = Integer.parseInt(secondLine[i]);\n        }\n        int maxWin = Math.min(game[0], game[4]) + Math.min(game[1], game[5]) + Math.min(game[2], game[3]);\n        int minWin = n - (Math.min(game[0], game[3] + game[5]) + Math.min(game[1], game[4] + game[3]) + Math.min(game[2], game[4] + game[5]));\n        System.out.println(minWin + \" \" + maxWin);\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class rps {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String[] firstLine = br.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(firstLine[0]);\n        String[] secondLine = br.readLine().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n        int[] alice = new int[3];\n        int[] bob = new int[3];\n        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n            alice[i] = Integer.parseInt(secondLine[i]);\n        }\n        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n            bob[i] = Integer.parseInt(secondLine[i + 3]);\n        }\n        \n        // Maximum wins for Alice:\n        // Alice wins when:\n        //   Alice rock (0) beats Bob scissors (1) -> bob[1]\n        //   Alice scissors (1) beats Bob paper (2) -> bob[2]\n        //   Alice paper (2) beats Bob rock (0) -> bob[0]\n        int maxWin = Math.min(alice[0], bob[1]) + Math.min(alice[1], bob[2]) + Math.min(alice[2], bob[0]);\n        \n        // Minimum wins for Alice:\n        // We want to minimize Alice's wins, which means maximizing:\n        // - Draws (same moves)\n        // - Bob's wins (Alice loses)\n        // Total rounds = n\n        // Alice wins + Bob wins + Draws = n\n        // So minimum Alice wins = n - (maximum possible draws + maximum possible Bob wins)\n        \n        // Maximum draws = alice[0] & bob[0] + alice[1] & bob[1] + alice[2] & bob[2]\n        int maxDraws = Math.min(alice[0], bob[0]) + Math.min(alice[1], bob[1]) + Math.min(alice[2], bob[2]);\n        \n        // Maximum Bob wins:\n        // Bob wins when:\n        //   Bob rock (0) beats Alice scissors (1) -> alice[1]\n        //   Bob scissors (1) beats Alice paper (2) -> alice[2]\n        //   Bob paper (2) beats Alice rock (0) -> alice[0]\n        int maxBobWins = Math.min(bob[0], alice[1]) + Math.min(bob[1], alice[2]) + Math.min(bob[2], alice[0]);\n        \n        // But we cannot simply add maxDraws and maxBobWins because they might overlap in assignments\n        // Instead, minimum Alice wins = n - (maximum non-wins for Alice)\n        // Non-wins for Alice = draws + Bob wins\n        // The maximum non-wins is n - minWins, so minWins = n - maxNonWins\n        \n        // Actually, we can compute minimum wins directly:\n        // For each of Alice's moves, we want to assign Bob's moves that don't result in Alice winning\n        // Alice's rock (0) can be matched with Bob's rock (0) or Bob's paper (2) - not Bob's scissors (1)\n        // Alice's scissors (1) can be matched with Bob's scissors (1) or Bob's rock (0) - not Bob's paper (2)\n        // Alice's paper (2) can be matched with Bob's paper (2) or Bob's scissors (1) - not Bob's rock (0)\n        \n        // So minimum wins = total rounds - maximum non-wins\n        // Maximum non-wins = \n        //   min(alice[0], bob[0] + bob[2]) + \n        //   min(alice[1], bob[1] + bob[0]) + \n        //   min(alice[2], bob[2] + bob[1])\n        \n        int maxNonWins = Math.min(alice[0], bob[0] + bob[2]) + \n                         Math.min(alice[1], bob[1] + bob[0]) + \n                         Math.min(alice[2], bob[2] + bob[1]);\n        \n        int minWin = n - maxNonWins;\n        \n        System.out.println(minWin + \" \" + maxWin);\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.1920922133867862e-06, -3.123234637314454e-05, -4.291525328881107e-06, 0.0, -2.1815061700181104e-05, -2.9444261599564925e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -6.556489552167477e-06, -6.246371776796877e-05, -1.5020257706055418e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -3.2066785934148356e-05, -4.6491513785440475e-06, -4.291525328881107e-06, 0.0, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -2.145764938177308e-06, 0.0, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -4.768360213347478e-06, -1.6927575416048057e-05, -5.960462772236497e-07, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -5.006777428206988e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -4.565611743601039e-05, -1.1205610462639015e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -4.291525328881107e-06, -1.2516897186287679e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, 0.0, -5.006777428206988e-06, -1.6212332411669195e-05, -2.288792165927589e-05, -1.883488948806189e-05, -6.198863957251888e-06, -2.5629668016335927e-05, -7.510157047363464e-06, -1.5616295058862306e-05, -6.782778655178845e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -5.722029527532868e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -5.8412379075889476e-06, -3.4570634852570947e-06, -3.039790681214071